{"text":"The [paper](https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/301348/3/301348.pdf) defines α=k/(N−1), with N real catalog items, and β=α[t(1−1/α)+1/α]=1−t+tα. Thus t=0 gives BCE (β=1); t=1 gives β=α. The exponent applies to the positive sigmoid: log(σ(s⁺)^β)=β log σ(s⁺), not log σ(βs⁺). Per valid sequence position, loss is [β·softplus(−s⁺)+Σⱼ softplus(s⁻ⱼ)]/(k+1). The [official training code](https://github.com/asash/gSASRec-pytorch/blob/main/train_gsasrec.py) averages over all k+1 logits, then over valid positions. Separately averaging positive and negative losses changes their relative weights.

A mathematically equivalent, numerically stable implementation is `(beta * F.softplus(-pos) + F.softplus(neg).sum(-1)) / (k + 1)`, followed by masked averaging; use FP32 or higher precision. This avoids sigmoid, powers, clipping and cancellation. The official implementation instead transforms positive logits in float64 with clamps: γ=−log(exp(β·softplus(−s⁺))−1), then applies BCEWithLogits. A stable log-space transformation uses `a=beta*softplus(-s⁺)` and γ=−log(expm1(a)); for large a, use γ=−a−log1p(−exp(−a)). Direct softplus loss is simpler.

For 128 replacement draws, count every duplicate: k=128 and denominator=129; uniqueness probability is not the expected multiplicity. However, the [official sampler](https://github.com/asash/gSASRec-pytorch/blob/main/dataset_utils.py) samples the entire catalog with replacement, without history exclusion. Excluding histories therefore changes its sampling assumptions. Derived interpretation: with M eligible items, expected multiplicity is 128/M, while inclusion probability is 1−(1−1/M)^128. Neither restores the paper’s global calibration guarantee because excluded items have zero negative exposure. For reproduction retain α=128/(N−1), documenting the sampler deviation. With replacement, M<128 still permits 128 draws; without replacement use actual k=min(128,M), update normalization, and skip M=0."}
